<Project>

  <!--
    Shared build settings for the SEL LIVE Windows Agent.

    ── Why .NET Framework and not .NET 8 ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    The estate includes Windows 7 SP1 machines. .NET 8 does not support Windows 7 at all, and
    WinUI 3 / MSIX need Windows 10 1809 or later — so a modern-stack agent would simply not run on
    a large part of the fleet. .NET Framework 4.8 with WPF runs unchanged from Windows 7 SP1 to
    Windows 11, is servicing-supported, and is already present in-box on Windows 10 1903+ and
    Windows 11. Nothing about that choice reaches the server: the agent speaks HTTPS and JSON to
    `/api/windows-agent/*`, and those routes neither know nor care what framework called them.

    ── The Windows 8.0 caveat, stated plainly ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    .NET Framework 4.8's supported client operating systems are **Windows 7 SP1, Windows 8.1,
    Windows 10 and Windows 11**. Windows 8.0 is *not* among them — Microsoft dropped it after
    4.6.1, and 8.0 itself left support in January 2016, when Microsoft made 8.1 a prerequisite for
    further updates.

    So a genuine Windows 8.0 machine cannot run this agent as configured, and the installer says so
    rather than failing obscurely. Two ways out, in order of preference:

      1. Update that PC to Windows 8.1. Free, and it is the supported configuration regardless of
         this agent — an unpatched 8.0 machine has had no security updates for a decade, which is
         a poor place to install software that handles credentials.

      2. Retarget the whole solution by overriding one property:

             dotnet build windows/SEL.Agent.sln -p:SelAgentTargetFramework=net461

         .NET Framework 4.6.1 supports Windows 8.0, and every dependency here (Newtonsoft.Json,
         System.Data.SQLite, Microsoft.Toolkit.Uwp.Notifications) still resolves at that level.
         What is lost is four years of framework servicing, so it is a deliberate trade and not
         the default.

    ── Language level ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    C# 7.3 — the last version with no runtime dependencies beyond .NET Framework. Newer language
    versions do compile against net48, but features like records and `init` need types the
    framework does not ship, and the resulting "add a shim so the compiler stops complaining"
    pattern is exactly the kind of thing that makes a legacy codebase hard to maintain on the old
    Visual Studio an engineer at a site office might be running. Plain C# costs nothing here.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <SelAgentTargetFramework Condition="'$(SelAgentTargetFramework)' == ''">net48</SelAgentTargetFramework>
    <TargetFramework>$(SelAgentTargetFramework)</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>7.3</LangVersion>

    <!--
      x86, on purpose, and not AnyCPU.

      The agent loads a native SQLite library and P/Invokes a good deal of user32/kernel32. An
      AnyCPU build runs 64-bit on a 64-bit PC and 32-bit on a 32-bit one, which means the fleet
      needs both sets of native binaries deployed and both code paths exercised. An x86 build runs
      identically everywhere under WOW64, and none of the Win32 calls this agent makes care —
      GetForegroundWindow and GetLastInputInfo are not affected by the caller's bitness, and
      reading a 64-bit process's executable path from a 32-bit caller works through
      QueryFullProcessImageName, which is why the code uses it rather than GetModuleFileNameEx.
    -->
    <PlatformTarget>x86</PlatformTarget>
    <Prefer32Bit>false</Prefer32Bit>

    <!--
      Bump this for every build anybody outside the developer's machine will install.

      It is not cosmetic. AgentVersion.Current is read from the assembly, travels on every
      heartbeat, and is what the fleet list and the auto-update check compare — so while every
      build said 1.0.0.0, "is the latest build on that PC?" had no answer, and an update could
      not be offered to a machine that needed one. Two separate afternoons were lost to that
      question before this was bumped.

      Keep the three numbers here, Package.wxs and build.ps1's -Version default in step; the
      installer's file name comes from the last of those.
    -->
    <AssemblyVersion>1.1.0.0</AssemblyVersion>
    <FileVersion>1.1.0.0</FileVersion>
    <Version>1.1.0</Version>
    <Company>Siddhartha Engineering Limited</Company>
    <Product>SEL LIVE Windows Agent</Product>
    <Copyright>© Siddhartha Engineering Limited</Copyright>

    <!-- Warnings are not errors here: net48 reference assemblies raise a handful of obsolete-API
         notices that are unavoidable on the legacy target and would otherwise block the build. -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>false</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <GenerateAssemblyInfo>true</GenerateAssemblyInfo>
    <AutoGenerateBindingRedirects>true</AutoGenerateBindingRedirects>
    <GenerateBindingRedirectsOutputType>true</GenerateBindingRedirectsOutputType>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Lets the .NET SDK build net48 without Visual Studio or a Developer Pack installed, which
         is what makes this solution buildable on a CI runner and on this workstation. -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NETFramework.ReferenceAssemblies" Version="1.0.3" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
